  m_Name: AshokaLion
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ARMuseum.Exhibits.ExhibitData
  title: ASHOKA LION CAPITAL
  subtitle: Lion capital in the Mauryan style, c. 3rd century BCE
  category: "Indian Heritage \u2022 Mauryan Sculpture"
  description: A sandstone lion capital in the Mauryan style, associated with the reign of Emperor Ashoka, who ruled most of the Indian subcontinent from about 268 to 232 BCE. Mauryan lion capitals crowned monolithic pillars that Ashoka had erected across his empire, carrying his edicts on Buddhist ethics and governance.
  symbolism:
  - label: Lions
    meaning: Lions are a traditional symbol of royal power and courage. In Buddhist texts the Buddha is sometimes called the lion of the Shakya clan.
  - label: Wheel on the abacus
    meaning: A wheel (dharmachakra) is often associated with the Buddha's teaching. The interpretation of the carving on this model is not established in this exhibit.
  - label: Four directions
    meaning: Four lions set back to back are often read as proclaiming the dharma in all directions. This reading is widely cited but not certain.
  - label: Polished surface
    meaning: The highly polished finish is typical of Mauryan stone sculpture and reflects the skill of its craftsmen.
  historicalContext: The Mauryan Empire, founded in the late 4th century BCE, reached its greatest extent under Ashoka. After his conversion to Buddhism, he promoted the dharma through edicts carved in stone and set up on pillars at important sites. Lion capitals of this kind crowned such pillars and are among the celebrated surviving works of Mauryan sculpture, known for their polished surfaces and strong, stylised forms.
  provenanceNote: This digital model is a reproduction. It is not identified as the Sarnath capital or any other specific original. The findspot, museum attribution and acquisition details are not confirmed for this exhibit. The 3D model and textures come from the supplied source files.
  narration: This is the Ashoka Lion Capital. Lion capitals like this one crowned pillars that Emperor Ashoka had raised across his empire in the third century BCE, carrying his edicts on Buddhist ethics and good governance. Look at the lions' powerful forms, and the polished stone that has survived for more than two thousand years.
  narrationClip: {fileID: 0}
  exhibitId: ashoka-lion-capital
  thumbnail: {fileID: 0}
  artifactPrefab: {fileID: 3145003022757412050, guid: f51c75864211cd74888e7096a38683f8, type: 3}
  displayHeightMeters: 0.4
  period: c. 3rd century BCE, Mauryan style
  introduction: A sandstone lion capital in the Mauryan style, of the kind associated with the reign of Emperor Ashoka, who ruled most of the Indian subcontinent from about 268 to 232 BCE.
  culturalSignificance: Ashoka's edicts, carved on rock and pillars, are often cited as among the earliest surviving records of an Indian ruler addressing his subjects directly, promoting ethics, tolerance and good governance. Pillars topped with lion capitals were widely understood as visible expressions of imperial authority across the empire.
  detailImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: e28a92e27b5e57e48a47b20e74ec7b14, type: 3}
